WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.810
To mark the release of Hotel Transylvania
2, let’s take a look at 12 things you probably

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:08.809
didn’t know about the animated monster movie.

00:00:08.809 --> 00:00:15.009
Director Genndy Tartakovsky was pretty nervous
about directing comedy legend Mel Brooks,

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:20.710
who voices Dracula’s father, Vlad, because
he grew up loving the comedian’s movies.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:26.210
Indeed, some of the first movies Tartakovsky
saw after he moved from Russia to America

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.840
as a child in the late 1970s were Mel Brooks’
films such as Young Frankenstein and Blazing

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:30.840
Saddles.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:37.699
And Brooks’ movies certainly came in handy
for the filmmakers, as they used them as reference

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:40.660
material for drawing Vlad’s reactions and
expressions.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:46.300
As for Mel Brooks, well he drew much of his
inspiration in bringing Vlad to life on screen

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.440
from his own grandfather.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.350
For the design of the monster camp, Camp Vamp,
the filmmakers took inspiration from two very

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.900
different places, which together gave them
the right look for the kind of dark and private

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.640
outdoor place vampires would enjoy.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:07.110
The first was Yosemite National Park and its
huge rock formations including El Capitán.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:11.860
And the second was the Norwegian town of Rjukan,
which used to spend half the year cut off

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.210
from sunlight because of its location in a
valley.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:18.740
Until, that is, huge mirrors were placed on
the surrounding mountain to reflect sunlight

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.680
into the town square.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.880
When the character designers first created
Mavis and Johnny’s son, Dennis, they drew

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.580
him with a big mane of red hair, like his
father.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:33.310
But when they turned that design into a computer
animation, they found Dennis’ hair looked

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.420
too big for his head to hold!

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:40.560
In fact, Dennis’ hair took up so much room
that Mavis or Johnny would have needed arms

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.070
twice the length in order to pick him up;
and, on top of that, his mass of curls kept

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:47.000
the light from reaching his face.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.140
So, the animators gave Dennis a haircut!

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.810
Six-year-old Asher Blinkoff, who is the voice
of Dracula’s grandson Dennis, isn’t the

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.590
only member of his family to appear in Hotel
Transylvania 2.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.500
Asher’s older sister, Meira, not only voiced
one of the werewolf puppies in the first movie,

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:11.050
but she’s also doing some additional voices
in the sequel including vampire kids.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.760
Asher was especially excited to get the role
as Dennis because whenever he or his sister

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.370
gets a part, it’s a family tradition to
go out for a sushi dinner!

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.280
Also, according to Asher’s mum, one of the
highlights for her son of working on Hotel

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:30.230
Transylvania 2 was riding in a golf cart around
the Sony Pictures studio lot!

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:35.920
Dracula’s daughter, Mavis, was designed
by French artist Anette Marnat, who’s based

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:36.920
in Paris.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:41.650
The filmmakers never actually met Anette in
real life, but thanks to her blog they discovered

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:47.780
her work when they were searching for character
designers for the first Hotel Transylvania.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.500
The Hotel Transylvania movies are a bit of
a family affair for Adam Sandler, who voices

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.500
Dracula.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:58.620
On the first movie, his real-life wife Jackie
provided the voice of his on-screen wife Martha,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:03.709
and his daughters Sunny and Sadie also lent
their voices to the film, with Sadie voicing

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:07.220
young Mavis and one of the werewolf pups,
Winnie.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.040
And Sunny and Sadie have returned for Hotel
Transylvania 2.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.489
Sadie is back as werewolf Winnie, while this
time Sunny is the voice of Baby Dennis, as

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:19.819
well as one of the vampire kids.

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:23.900
When Character Designer Carter Goodrich was
looking for inspiration for the design of

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:28.069
Griffin the Invisible Man, who’s voiced
by actor David Spade, he didn’t have to

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:34.460
look any further than his own nose, as Griffin’s
glasses are based on the ones Goodrich wears

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:35.760
himself!

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.060
The hairstyle of Frankenstein’s wife, Eunice,
is based on a Brillo pad!

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.360
And Eunice’s hair isn’t the only rough
thing about the character.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.700
According to Fran Drescher, who voices Eunice,
during her recording sessions the filmmakers

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:53.890
kept encouraging her to make Eunice sound
gruffer, more obnoxious, and annoying!

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:59.150
Barcelona-based designer Carlos Grangel, who
contributed to the look of many of the movie’s

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:02.599
characters including Murray the Mummy, hates
designing on white paper.

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:08.440
So, instead, he drew all his monster designs
on the inside of cereal boxes, which he prefers

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:13.200
to blank paper because it has more of colour
and texture to it, which helps him get creative!

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.820
In fact, Grangel asks fellow artists at his
studio in Spain to bring in their old cereal

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.829
boxes for him to draw on.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:26.870
Andy Samberg, who voices Dracula’s son-in-law
Jonathan, has said he based his character

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:32.089
on himself in high school "and on a lot of
guys he knew in the Bay Area in California."

00:04:32.089 --> 00:04:37.120
Jonathan loves seeing the world and is super-positive
when faced with adversity.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:43.729
When Sony was designing characters for Hotel
Transylvania, they had to be careful about

00:04:43.729 --> 00:04:45.150
copyright issues.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:50.529
Although characters such as Dracula and Frankenstein’s
monster are in the public domain, Universal

00:04:50.529 --> 00:04:55.560
created specific designs for those characters
in their popular horror movies of the 1930s

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:56.560
onwards.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.069
So, Sony had to avoid certain specific features
associated with Universal’s classic monsters.

00:05:02.069 --> 00:05:08.280
For example, Frankenstein could not have green
skin or bolts in his neck, and Dracula could

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:12.479
not have a sharp widow’s peak or a cape
with a red lining.

00:05:12.479 --> 00:05:17.669
Also, instead of calling Frankenstein’s
mate the Bride of Frankenstein, they called

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:22.900
her Eunice, and on top of that, she could
not have two white lightning bolts in her

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.110
hair, so in Hotel Transylvania, she has just
one streak instead.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:32.020
